  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure moving into the area from the west has brought\nsunny skies and NW winds that are continuing to push cool, dry\nair into the region from Canada. These winds are mixing dry air\nto the surface, with dewpoints currently in the mid 20s to low\n30s. Temperatures will top out in the mid to upper 50s today. \n\nAnother cold night is expected across the area tonight. The dry\nair and clear skies will allow temperatures to quickly fall \ninto the mid to upper 30s by late evening. The ridge axis and \nassociated surface high will push east of the area as the night \nprogresses, allowing for southerly flow to return and increase \nwinds in the lower atmosphere from west to east starting around \n10pm. This should keep frost from forming mostly west of I-81. \nEast of I-81, the high should have enough influence to generate \nareas of frost. With PA still in the growing season, a Frost \nAdvisory has been issued for Susquehanna, Wayne and Pike \ncounties from 11pm tonight to 9am. The Wyoming Valley looks to \nbe too warm with several ensemble models showing a near 0% \nchance of temps falling far enough for frost to form. Higher \nelevations outside of the valley and to the NW into Bradford \ncounty could see patchy frost develop across sheltered areas. \nWarm weather returns for Wednesday, with highs in the mid to \nupper 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nMostly warm and pretty quite weather expected through the \nweekend. The only weathermaker will be a weak trough sliding \nacross Ontario Wednesday into Thursday, bringing scattered light\nrain showers across northern portions of the area Wednesday \nafternoon into Thursday. A cold front will move through Thursday\nnight, dropping lows into the low 40s Thursday night and Friday\nnight with Friday highs in the low to mid 60s. Highs in the mid\n60s to low 70s expected Wed, Thurs, Sat and Sun. \n\nWe are monitoring a tropical system developing in the Gulf of\nAmerica. Some model guidance brings rain into the area sometime\nMonday. This is a complicated setup with a high over central\nCanada, a trough moving into the Pacific NW, and 2 tropical\nsystems in the eastern Pacific moving into the SW US impacting\nthe track and timing of anything coming out of the Gulf. With\nall of these factors, the forecast will have high uncertainty\nuntil later this week when hopefully models have resolved how\nsome of these aforementioned features will develop.",
